ssh-otp — single-use SSH passphrases
===================================

Build on the target Linux system with Zig 0.16.0 and its native C development
headers/toolchain:

    mise exec -- zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe
    mise exec -- zig build test -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe

Or invoke Zig 0.16.0 directly. There are no third-party Zig packages or package
fetches. Zig compiles the small included C ABI boundary for libc's stat, timespec
and rlimit types; authentication and credential management remain in Zig.
Python 3.10+ is used for installation, not login. OpenSSH with Linux-PAM support
and the system PAM runtime are required.

Distribution profiles
---------------------

The installer detects /etc/os-release and chooses an audited profile:

    Debian / Ubuntu     common-auth; ssh.service (sshd alias)
    Fedora / RHEL       password-auth / postlogin; sshd.service
    Arch Linux          system-remote-login / system-login / system-auth;
                        sshd.service
    Alpine Linux        base-auth; OpenRC sshd; PAM-capable sshd.pam executable

Install build/runtime prerequisites with your distribution's package manager.
Typical package sets (not an instruction to replace existing authentication):

    Debian/Ubuntu:  build-essential openssh-server libpam-modules libpam-runtime python3
    Fedora:         gcc glibc-devel openssh-server pam authselect python3
    Arch:           base-devel openssh pam pambase python
    Alpine:         build-base openssh-server-pam openssh-server-common-openrc
                    linux-pam python3

Unknown distributions are not guessed from a vaguely related ID_LIKE value.
An administrator may explicitly choose --profile debian|fedora|arch|alpine after
reviewing the actual layout. Profiles recursively inspect their authentication
includes, retain recognized authorization gates and preserve root account,
session and password-management rules. Unknown auth modules, custom MFA,
unsupported control flow, include cycles and hidden password fallbacks are
rejected rather than silently removed. System-wide PAM/authselect files are read,
not rewritten; only the SSH service PAM file is changed.

Profiles are not a promise that every customized PAM stack, service manager,
architecture or distribution derivative works. Primary packaging sources and
fixture provenance are in tests/fixtures/<profile>/SOURCE.

Install
-------

First establish working SSH-key access and keep that trusted session open.
The supported starting SSH configuration is key-only authentication, with
PasswordAuthentication no and KbdInteractiveAuthentication no. Existing
multi-factor AuthenticationMethods policies are not silently replaced.

    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk

Build before using sudo; do not build as host root. Installation requires an
explicit --user to check effective SSH settings; this does not restrict burner
issuance to that account. Account creation, AllowUsers/AllowGroups, root-login
restrictions and sudo policy remain the administrator's responsibility.

The CLI is root-owned mode 4755. The PAM module and runtime store are root-controlled.
The CLI filesystem must permit setuid execution. SSH's ordinary password method
stays disabled; the separate keyboard-interactive PAM method accepts burners.
The installer requires an effective /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf include and
validates that its drop-in actually takes effect. If configuration conflicts,
it rolls back rather than guessing where to insert authentication directives.

Optional overrides:

    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk --profile arch
    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk --sshd-path /usr/sbin/sshd --service sshd

The installer discovers a trusted sshd executable and the loaded systemd SSH
unit, or OpenRC's SSH service. It reloads, never restarts or unexpectedly starts
a service. A socket-activated but inactive service may require administrator
activation before reload; automatic installation will roll back on reload failure.
Only the selected user's localhost SSH context is checked. Administrators must
review additional Match blocks and external configuration includes.

Use --no-reload for offline/staged installation and reload the correct service
when ready:

    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk --no-reload
    sudo systemctl reload ssh       # Debian/Ubuntu
    sudo systemctl reload sshd      # Fedora/Arch
    sudo rc-service sshd reload     # Alpine/OpenRC

Alpine: the default sshd binary is not PAM-capable. Install openssh-server-pam;
the installer uses sshd.pam. OpenRC can select it when UsePAM is enabled, but
reloading an already-running non-PAM executable cannot turn it into sshd.pam.
The installer refuses that case. Migrate the daemon through a trusted session
before installing; it will not restart your SSH service to perform the migration.

SELinux: a rootless container cannot validate enforcing domain transitions or
runtime-store permissions. On enforcing hosts, installation refuses by default.
Provision and test a policy for the actual SSH authentication domain, the issuer,
and /run/ssh-otp, including persistent file contexts/creation labels. Only then
use --selinux-policy-reviewed to acknowledge that administrator-supplied policy.
The installer applies restorecon after file replacement when SELinux is active;
this does not create policy or prove the credential store is accessible. It never
disables SELinux, makes it permissive, or grants broad permissions automatically.

Keep the trusted session open until a second session succeeds. Installation and
uninstallation attempt rollback on errors, including failures during backup or
cleanup. Recovery failures are reported separately from the original error;
recovery files are retained when rollback cannot finish. A PAM module still
required by the active configuration is not removed during failed recovery.
SIGKILL or power loss may require manual recovery using
/var/lib/ssh-otp-install/sshd.pam.original; transactions are not power-loss-atomic.
Installation with existing state is refused. Uninstall before reinstalling.

Usage
-----

From a trusted server session:

    ssh-otp hayk 10m

On the standard Windows SSH client:

    ssh hayk@server

If necessary, explicitly select the authentication method:

    ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard-interactive hayk@server

No client download or SSH key is needed on Windows. Verify the server host-key
fingerprint through the trusted session before accepting it on the client.

Only existing users are supported. Non-root issuers can target only their real
UID; root can target any existing account. SSH account restrictions still apply,
so issuance does not guarantee the account is allowed to log in.

Four uniformly random EFF words are joined by '=' (about 51.7 bits of entropy).
Four EFF entries contain hyphens, which are retained. The phrase is shown once,
only to a terminal, and is never stored in plaintext. Terminal scrollback may
retain it. One phrase can be outstanding per UID; a new issuance replaces it.
Five incorrect candidates invalidate a phrase, so an attacker can cause denial
of service to an issuance.

Without an active burner, the module rejects before asking for a password.
Missing, expired, consumed and revoked burners receive no burner-specific
explanation. The SSH method remains configured; key authentication is unchanged.
Preserved distro authorization gates can still enforce and report their own policy.

Duration is an integer with s/m/h suffix from 1s to 1h. The boot clock includes
suspend, with one-second precision. Authentication consumes the phrase atomically,
even if later account checks fail or the client disconnects. Established sessions
continue after expiry. The burner is not a sudo credential.

Ctrl-C, SIGTERM and SIGHUP revoke outstanding credentials. A killed/crashed issuer
may leave one active until its original deadline; PAM enforces expiry independently.
Expired records are removed on access or reboot. The /run store is not persistent.

An untrusted client can steal the phrase before use, control the session and
capture a reusable sudo password. This avoids leaving reusable SSH keys behind;
it does not make a compromised client trustworthy. Do not forward an SSH agent.
This is new privileged authentication code, not an independently audited product.

Uninstall
---------

    sudo python3 install.py uninstall

Uninstall removes the SSH drop-in and reloads before restoring the original PAM
file and removing binaries. This avoids enabling password-backed PAM while the
running daemon still advertises burner authentication. It refuses files whose
contents, root ownership or expected modes changed. Existing hash manifests remain
valid; modes are checked against the fixed installation policy.
Use --no-reload for uninstall only when SSH is stopped (as in the container tests).
Outstanding burners are revoked, including potentially during a failed uninstall
whose files were successfully restored. Existing sessions are not disconnected.

Verification
------------

Fast profile/parser tests:

    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_profile_*.py'
    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_sshd_settings.py

Installer failure-path tests use temporary fixtures and injected I/O failures.
Run as your normal user with subordinate UID/GID mappings (uidmap), not host root:

    unshare --user --map-auto --map-root-user \
        python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_install.py -v

The existing Debian namespace scenario uses the host's OpenSSH/PAM libraries:

    unshare --user --map-auto --map-root-user --mount --net \
        python3 tests/integration.py

For native distro verification, use rootless Podman (no Docker daemon, no VM,
no init-system boot). Each minimal image installs its actual OpenSSH/PAM packages,
compiles the source with the mounted Zig toolchain, then exercises real key and
burner SSH logins on private loopback with a pinned host key:

    mise exec -- python3 tests/containers.py debian
    mise exec -- python3 tests/containers.py ubuntu
    mise exec -- python3 tests/containers.py fedora
    mise exec -- python3 tests/containers.py arch
    mise exec -- python3 tests/containers.py alpine

Alternatively pass --zig-dir /path/to/zig-installation (contains zig and lib/).
Use --rebuild to refresh base images/packages. Container runtime networking is
none; no ports are published and source/toolchain mounts are read-only. Containers
are removed afterward; test images and per-distro Zig cache volumes remain cached.
Build packages/toolchain consume more storage than the idle SSH daemon: minimal
here means isolated processes without a full OS boot, not zero build dependencies.

These tests check native source compilation, credential lifecycle, real packaged
PAM account/session policy, key and burner login, session survival past expiry,
replay and Unix-password rejection, prompt-free inactive rejection, and original
PAM restoration. They do NOT boot/test systemd or OpenRC reloads, nor establish
SELinux enforcement compatibility. RHEL-derived layouts are source-fixture
verified; Fedora is the runtime representative, not a substitute for RHEL tests.

